/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  edbatch03_multiionic_split  --  a batch electrodialysis rig on a MIXED brine,
  and two counter-ions on each membrane sharing its current.

  THE OPERATION.  The rig of edbatch01 (Eurodia EUR2C-7P18, seven cell pairs,
  175 L/h recirculation), but the diluate tank holds 0.05 mol/kg NaCl plus
  0.025 mol/kg K2SO4 -- two cations (Na+, K+) on the cation-exchange
  membrane and two anions (Cl-, SO4 2-) on the anion-exchange one.  The
  rectifier is held at 2.0 A (100 A/m2 on one cell pair).

  THE RULE (Vitor's ruling C35, 2026-10-05).  Until that day this unit
  REFUSED a diluate with more than one cation or one anion, for want of a
  rule dividing a membrane's current between its counter-ions.  The ruling
  shares it by

      t_k = |z_k| D_k c_k / SUM_j |z_j| D_j c_j      over that membrane's counter-ions

  -- the transport numbers of Geraldes & Afonso, J. Membr. Sci. 360 (2010)
  499-508, Eqs. (12)/(13), applied at every current and not only at the
  limit -- with D the ions' curated D0 and NO new parameter.  Each ion then
  leaves at t_k xi I N / (|z_k| F) mol/s, so each membrane moves exactly
  xi I N / F equivalents and both tanks stay electroneutral.

  WHAT A STUDENT SHOULD SEE.  Divide that rate by the ion's own inventory:

      d ln n_k / dt  =  - D_k xi I N / ( F SUM_j |z_j| D_j n_j )

  -- the FRACTIONAL depletion rate of a counter-ion is proportional to its
  diffusivity and to nothing else about it, so on a log scale the
  counter-ions of one membrane fall along straight lines whose slopes stand
  in the ratio of their D0.  K+ (1.96e-9 m2/s) is depleted 1.47 times faster
  than Na+ (1.33e-9) in log terms; Cl- (2.03e-9) 1.92 times faster than
  SO4 2- (1.06e-9), the charge of the sulfate making no difference to its
  FRACTIONAL rate (it carries twice the current per mole and so moves half
  the moles per coulomb, exactly compensating its doubled share).  The run
  publishes `demin_<ion>` for every ion and `t_transfer_final_<ion>`, the
  shares at the end, and its trajectory carries every molality.

  WHAT THE RUN MEASURES (every number is printed by this run; the golden
  pins them):
      i                       100.00 A/m2, flat, all 2400 s
      i_lim                   696.83 A/m2 at t = 0, 244.39 at t = 2400 s
                              (i/i_lim 0.144 -> 0.409: never crossed)
      removed at t = 2400 s   K+ 69.77 %   Na+ 55.60 %      (CEM)
                              Cl- 74.43 %  SO4 2- 50.94 %   (AEM)
      share of the current    Na+ 0.404 -> 0.499 of the CEM's
                              Cl- 0.657 -> 0.500 of the AEM's
      material closure        0 on mass, 1.4e-15 on the worst element

  READ THE SHARES BACKWARDS AND THE LESSON IS THERE.  At t = 0 the two
  cations are equally concentrated, so K+, the faster, takes 0.596 of the
  cation current; it therefore depletes faster, its c falls, and the share
  drifts toward Na+.  The hand calculation `demin_ideal` (Faraday's law at
  the INITIAL current AND the reference cation's INITIAL share, computed
  from the run) predicts 50.68 % of the sodium removed; the run removes
  55.60 %, because sodium's share of the current GROWS as potassium is
  stripped -- the opposite sign from edbatch01's gap, and for a different
  reason.  And ln(n/n0)/D0 comes out as one number for K+ and Na+, and one
  number for Cl- and SO4 2-, to the integrator's precision.

  WHAT IS PINNED AND WHAT IS NOT.  The golden pins the answer THIS model
  gives.  The share z D c is a RULING, not a measurement: real ion-exchange
  membranes add a preference between counter-ions of their own (a Donnan
  preference for divalent ions, or a monovalent-selective layer) which no
  `kind IEM` record in this tree carries and this unit does not model.  The
  brine is an author-set assumption; nothing here is validated against a
  measured rig.  The membrane potential of a membrane with two counter-ions
  is taken as the MEAN of their own Nernst potentials (announced).  Back-
  diffusion, water transport and every thermal effect are not modelled --
  the unit's own header lists them.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        rig;
        type        batchElectrodialysis;

        operation
        {
            stack          EUR2C-7P18;   // the HARDWARE, as a record (ed03)
            diluateFlow    175 L/h;      // the pump setting (edbatch01's)
            //  CONSTANT CURRENT.  100 A/m2 on one cell pair -- below i_lim
            //  for the whole run, so the over-limiting regime is never
            //  entered; the model is declared so that silence means that.
            current        2.0;           // A (raw SI)
            xi             0.9;           // current efficiency (declared)
            E_electrodes   1.5;           // V (raw SI), lumped, announced
            overLimiting   { model none; }
        }
    }
);
